Compling the Code:
==================

Choose one among the available configuration files to create your own 
definitions.hpp and pluto.ini, e.g.:

> cp definitions_01.hpp definitions.hpp
> cp pluto_01.ini       pluto.ini

Run the python script, 

> python $gPLUTO_DIR/setup.py

and confirm the present selection by pressing enter. 
Also, choose a suitable makefile, e.g. "linux.g++.defs".
If you use GNUPLOT to visualize data, before compiling, add

#define  GNUPLOT_HEADER                 YES

to your definitions.hpp.

Now you can simply type "make" at the command prompt.

Running the Code:
=================

The left and right states for the problem are defined inside "pluto.ini".
Se the frequency of tab/dbl output formats.
You can leave the deafault and simply run the code with 

> ./pluto 

at the command prompt (>).

Visualizing the data:
=====================

Data can be visualized using Gnuplot.
A script "shock_tube.gp" can be used to produce an animation throughout the
simulation data.
[Note: to be able to run the script, the environment variable 
 GNUPLOT_LIB should point to $gPLUTO_DIR/Tools/Gnuplot]

The variable to be visualized together with its name can be set under the
section "Plot Specifications".
Variable names (rho, prs, vx1, ...) are listed from "pluto.gp".



